WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.980
is we're going to have to automate faster.
I don't know if this automation can happen

00:03.980 --> 00:07.920
quickly enough. The meat facilities are
right now running under capacity.

00:11.080 --> 00:15.280
Beef is up about 24%, depending on which
article you read. Much of it is due, you

00:15.280 --> 00:18.500
can say, because of packer control, but
also because of the human hands that touch

00:18.500 --> 00:22.880
our meat in the facilities. Can we
automate fast enough? To do so, I believe

00:22.880 --> 00:27.400
that we're going to see more ag innovation
come from outside agriculture. What if the

00:27.400 --> 00:32.340
new innovation is beyond just giving an
engineer a task and they change it 10%,

00:32.340 --> 00:36.320
but instead they change it 100%?
Innovation will come to this industry from

00:36.320 --> 00:40.880
outside, and we need to embrace it, not
reject it. Because innovation that comes

00:40.880 --> 00:45.480
to agriculture might be from a completely
different 180-degree position, and you'll

00:45.480 --> 00:49.920
say, like, holy crap, I can't believe we
didn't do that. Uber and Lyft were not

00:49.920 --> 00:56.820
founded by the CEO of Yellow Cab, were
they? And I'm telling you what, I'm

00:56.820 --> 01:01.540
opposed to it. When I was a kid at
Northwest Elementary School, I remember

01:01.540 --> 01:05.460
the day that the policeman came in there
in kindergarten. The first thing he said

01:05.460 --> 01:12.560
was, "Children, don't ever get into a car
with strangers." Now it's a business.

01:12.560 --> 01:16.420
Think about that. You were told from the
time you were a kid, "Don't get in a car

01:16.420 --> 01:20.280
with a stranger." Now it's just to hop in
a car when somebody pulls up and says,

01:20.280 --> 01:25.000
"Are you Damon?" No. They can't get your
name right, and I hop in a car with them.

01:25.380 --> 01:26.500
Makes no sense to me.

01:26.580 --> 01:27.180
Thank you.

01:27.400 --> 01:34.400
[music]
